# Pytho’s Mask Glulx startup: read-only diagnosis

Snapshot: 2026-09-25T08:44:17.546661+00:00. Session `pythos-mask-a39eb2af1a`, actual Glulx R4/020223. Held slot preserved. No live input, interpreter probe, debugger attachment, reset, signal, save, app modification or config change. Only this report written.

## Findings

**The CLI is blocked on a pipe read, not visibly spending time executing the VM.** PID 473923 has one thread, sleeping state S and wait channel `anon_pipe_read` in repeated snapshots. Its fd 0 is the read end of pipe 13712520; the broker PID 473922 owns that pipe’s write end on fd 8. CLI fd 1 and broker fd 9 share output pipe 13712521. CLI fds are only stdin, stdout, stderr; broker is in `ep_poll`. No stderr bytes or exit evidence. `/proc/473923/syscall` returns EPERM: this audit cannot pin the exact syscall arguments or user-space/story call site. Broker-parent access can differ from this sibling auditor’s access; e1/e2 readiness receipts must not be discounted because my syscall access failed.

**Enter is being encoded as a Glk Return, not lost to trimming.** The current live executable SHA256 matches the on-disk gvm-cli, and read-only disassembly of `gvm_cli::read_char_input` shows the matching LF/CR-to-Return mapping. This narrows the failure to an unresolved input/read boundary or silent story response; it does not establish which key this game accepts at MORE.

**The broker cannot settle a zero-output input wait.** Its `buf` prerequisite skips readiness checking when no output was read; passive `await_output` uses the same logic. This is the same structural broker limitation identified in the earlier Z-machine menu diagnosis. It can explain the persistent timeout after a silently ignored key or a silent transition. There is no evidence here proving which happened, or that a redraw-deduplication issue is the cause in Pytho.

